From d51f76193caf01339fc0440a04266133c8bbbee5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Date: Fri, 12 Jun 2026 00:00:00 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] vhost_xen: Rebind guest domid on every device-model change

The watch callback vhost_xen_get_guest_domid() only updates the global
guest_domid when the "device-model" directory in the local Xenstore tree
contains exactly one entry (n == 1) and resets it only when the
directory is empty (n == 0). For n >= 2 it silently does nothing.

That guard is not enough on a disaggregated setup where Qemu runs in a
driver domain (DomD), because the toolstack never removes
/local/domain/<DomD>/device-model/<domid> when a guest is destroyed:
libxl__destroy_device_model() only removes the device-model path under
LIBXL_TOOLSTACK_DOMID (Dom0). Stale entries therefore accumulate, so as
soon as a second guest is created the directory holds two entries and
guest_domid stays latched to the very first guest -- which may already be
gone. Mapping then fails in vhost_xen_map_desc():

  vhost_xen_map_desc: Failed to map pages from dom2 (ret=-3)

because XENMEM_add_to_physmap_range (or grant mapping) targets the
destroyed domain (-ESRCH). The only recovery was a board reboot.

Scan every "device-model/<domid>" entry instead, and bind to the LOWEST
domid that both reports state "running" and still has a live
/local/domain/<domid> tree. The liveness check is what actually discards
the stale leftovers -- Xen removes a domain's Xenstore tree when the
domain goes away, whereas a stale device-model entry can keep a last
recorded state of "running", so no ordering of domids can distinguish
them. Reset guest_domid when no live running instance exists (including
the case where the whole directory disappears, which the old code also
failed to handle).

Lowest-first preserves the upstream selection semantics: with a single
device-model the original code latched that one guest and never rebound,
so the first-created vhost guest kept the binding for the life of the
boot. It also matches the only topology in which one DomD backs several
guests here -- DomA is created before DomU and is the sole user of vhost
(its AAOS VHAL rides vhost-vsock, while DomU's netdev is vhost=off and it
has no vsock device). Selecting the highest domid instead would hand the
binding to DomU, which uses no vhost device at all, and DomA's
vhost-vsock and vhost-net would map the wrong domain -- observed on
hardware as the AAOS guest getting no DHCP lease on either of its two
interfaces and its VHAL never reaching the host, which in turn makes
com.android.car crash-loop and leaves the AAOS panel black.

Two deliberate conservatism choices in the scan, both aimed at never making
things worse than the code being replaced:

 - The liveness probe treats only -ENOENT as "this domain is gone". Any
   other error means the probe could not be answered -- DomD here is built
   with DISTRO_FEATURES += vmsep, so xenstore VM separation can deny
   reading another domain's tree, and a non-reference xenstore server such
   as the Zephyr Dom0 one may answer differently -- and the candidate is
   then treated as live. An unanswerable probe must not be able to discard
   a valid candidate.

 - guest_domid is only reset to DOMID_INVALID when the device-model
   directory is genuinely empty. If it holds entries but none could be
   qualified, the last known good binding is kept and a warning is
   logged. Clearing it would break
   vhost_xen_map_desc() for every guest at once, which is worse than the
   n >= 2
   no-op it replaces.

Additionally expose guest_domid as a writable module parameter
(/sys/module/vhost_xen/parameters/guest_domid) so that the binding can
be inspected and manually corrected at runtime without rebooting the
board.

This keeps the single-active-guest model of the original EPAM design;
the proper long-term solution remains passing the guest domid from Qemu
through a VHOST ioctl and storing it per device.

Assisted-by: Claude Code:claude-opus-4-8
Signed-off-by: Yuichi Kusakabe <yuichi_kusakabe@jp.honda>
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/vhost/xen.c | 124 +-
 1 file changed, 99 insertions(+), 25 deletions(-)

diff --git a/drivers/vhost/xen.c b/drivers/vhost/xen.c
index 4d3927e73feb..8ccbb086e33e 100644
--- a/drivers/vhost/xen.c
+++ b/drivers/vhost/xen.c
@@ -23,6 +23,9 @@
 #include "vhost.h"
 
 static domid_t guest_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
+module_param(guest_domid, ushort, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(guest_domid,
+		 "Current guest domid used for Xen mappings (writable for manual recovery)");
 
 static bool nogrant;
 module_param(nogrant, bool, 0444);
@@ -412,43 +415,133 @@ static void vhost_xen_get_guest_domid(struct xenbus_watch *watch,
 		const char *path, const char *token)
 {
 	char **dm_dir;
-	unsigned int n = 0;
+	unsigned int i, n = 0;
+	domid_t new_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
 
 	dm_dir = xenbus_directory(XBT_NIL, "device-model", "", &n);
 	if (IS_ERR(dm_dir))
 		return;
 
-	if (n == 0) {
-		if (guest_domid != DOMID_INVALID) {
-			guest_domid = DOMID_INVALID;
-			pr_info("%s: Reset current domid\n", __func__);
-		}
-	} else if (n == 1) {
+	/*
+	 * Select the LOWEST domid whose device-model reports state "running"
+	 * AND whose domain still exists.
+	 *
+	 * Lowest-first matches both the upstream EPAM semantics (latch the single
+	 * device-model present at n == 1 and never rebind, so the first-created
+	 * vhost guest keeps the binding) and the topology this board ships: DomD
+	 * backs DomA and DomU, DomA is created first, and DomA is the only guest
+	 * that uses vhost at all -- its AAOS VHAL rides vhost-vsock, while DomU's
+	 * netdev is vhost=off and it has no vsock device. A highest-first rule
+	 * hands the binding to DomU, which uses no vhost device, and DomA's
+	 * vhost-vsock and vhost-net then map the wrong domain.
+	 *
+	 * The reason this callback scans at all, instead of keeping upstream's
+	 * "only act when n == 1" guard, is that the toolstack never removes
+	 * /local/domain/<DomD>/device-model/<domid> when a guest is destroyed:
+	 * libxl__destroy_device_model() only cleans the path under
+	 * LIBXL_TOOLSTACK_DOMID. Stale entries accumulate and can even keep a
+	 * last recorded state of "running", so ranking by domid cannot tell them
+	 * apart. Each candidate is therefore checked for liveness below: Xen
+	 * removes /local/domain/<domid> when the domain goes away.
+	 *
+	 * NOTE. The proper solution would be to pass the guest domid from Qemu
+	 * directly by extending some of VHOST ioctls and to keep it per device
+	 * instead of in a single global variable.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
-		char *dm_str, *dm_path;
-		domid_t domid;
+		char *dm_str, *dm_path, **dom_dir;
+		unsigned int m = 0;
+		domid_t domid;
 
-		domid = simple_strtoul(dm_dir[0], NULL, 0);
+		/*
+		 * A directory entry that is not a number is not a domid. The
+		 * simple_strtoul() this replaces parsed such an entry as 0 and
+		 * would have bound vhost to Dom0; skip it instead.
+		 */
+		if (kstrtou16(dm_dir[i], 0, &domid))
+			continue;
 
-		dm_path = kasprintf(GFP_KERNEL, "device-model/%d", domid);
+		dm_path = kasprintf(GFP_KERNEL, "device-model/%u", domid);
 		if (!dm_path)
-			goto out;
+			continue;
 
 		dm_str = (char *)xenbus_read(XBT_NIL, dm_path, "state", NULL);
-		if (IS_ERR(dm_str)) {
-			kfree(dm_path);
-			goto out;
-		}
+		kfree(dm_path);
+		if (IS_ERR(dm_str))
+			continue;
 
-		if (!strcmp(dm_str, "running")) {
-			guest_domid = domid;
-			pr_info("%s: Set new domid: %u\n", __func__, guest_domid);
-		}
+		if (strcmp(dm_str, "running")) {
+			kfree(dm_str);
+			continue;
+		}
+		kfree(dm_str);
+
+		/*
+		 * Liveness: Xen tears down /local/domain/<domid> when the
+		 * domain is destroyed, so a stale device-model entry left
+		 * behind by the toolstack fails this and is skipped.
+		 */
+		dm_path = kasprintf(GFP_KERNEL, "/local/domain/%u", domid);
+		if (!dm_path)
+			continue;
+
+		dom_dir = xenbus_directory(XBT_NIL, dm_path, "", &m);
+		kfree(dm_path);
+		if (!IS_ERR(dom_dir)) {
+			kfree(dom_dir);
+		} else if (PTR_ERR(dom_dir) == -ENOENT) {
+			/* Positively gone: Xen removed the tree. Skip. */
+			continue;
+		}
+		/*
+		 * Any other error (notably -EACCES: this DomD is built with
+		 * DISTRO_FEATURES += vmsep, so xenstore VM separation can deny
+		 * reading another domain's tree, and a non-reference xenstore
+		 * server such as the Zephyr Dom0 one may answer differently)
+		 * means we simply do not know. Treat the candidate as live: an
+		 * unanswerable probe must never make this callback discard a
+		 * working binding.
+		 */
+
+		/*
+		 * Lowest live domid wins. This encodes the boot order the
+		 * platform guarantees: DomA (the only vhost-vsock guest) is
+		 * created before DomU, so it always holds the lower domid, and
+		 * DomU's later device-model entry can no longer steal the
+		 * binding the way the previous highest-domid rule allowed.
+		 *
+		 * Known limitation: if DomA were destroyed and re-created while
+		 * DomU stayed up, DomA would come back with a HIGHER domid and
+		 * this rule would keep pointing at DomU. That case is out of
+		 * scope by design -- destroying DomA is not supported on this
+		 * platform (the single global binding below is exactly why) --
+		 * but a topology with several vhost guests would need the domid
+		 * to be a per-device property instead of a module-global.
+		 */
+		if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID || domid < new_domid)
+			new_domid = domid;
 
-		kfree(dm_path);
-		kfree(dm_str);
 	}
 
-out:
+	/*
+	 * Only tear the binding down when the directory is genuinely empty. If it
+	 * has entries but none of them could be qualified (every xenbus read
+	 * failed, a transient -ENOMEM, a transaction retry), keeping the last
+	 * known good binding is strictly better than clearing it: clearing makes
+	 * every subsequent vhost_xen_map_desc() fail for all guests at once.
+	 */
+	if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID && n > 0) {
+		if (guest_domid != DOMID_INVALID)
+			pr_warn("%s: no qualifying device-model among %u entries; keeping dom%u\n",
+				__func__, n, guest_domid);
+	} else if (new_domid != guest_domid) {
+		guest_domid = new_domid;
+		if (new_domid == DOMID_INVALID)
+			pr_info("%s: Reset current domid\n", __func__);
+		else
+			pr_info("%s: Set new domid: %u\n", __func__, guest_domid);
+	}
+
 	kfree(dm_dir);
 }
 
